WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.440
Stephen King's It is a complex story in which
a lot of things happen, and at times things

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.620
get a bit confusing.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:10.220
But don't worry about getting lost in the
story — we're here to explain everything

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:14.490
you need to understand the full story of the
movies made from the classic novel.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.620
Possible spoilers ahead.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:22.240
2017's It didn't get into the backstory of
its ancient antagonist outside of implying

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:26.529
that it's been around for a long time, but
thankfully, King's original novel goes into

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:27.580
much more detail.

00:00:27.909 --> 00:00:33.500
It, the shapeshifting being that often manifests
itself as a creepy clown named Pennywise,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:38.230
has actually been around longer than mankind
itself — It's an ancient cosmic being that's

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:43.170
billions of years old that originated in another
dimension outside of our universe known as

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:43.940
the Macroverse.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.900
In prehistoric times, It came to Earth on
an asteroid, landing in the place that would

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.640
millions of years later become Derry, Maine.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:57.460
It hibernated until humans appeared, and then
began a cycle of awakening roughly every 27

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:58.740
years to feed on them.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:03.900
It can take on any form, but its favorite
eventually became that of Pennywise the clown.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.700
Its true form, known as the Deadlights, is
apparently so trippy it'll drive you crazy.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.680
Beverly Marsh managed to glimpse them in the
first film and make it out with her sanity,

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.620
though doing so did render her comatose for
a time.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.520
As strange as Pennywise is, its mortal enemy
is even more bizarre: A giant turtle named

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.540
Maturin who hails from the same place as Pennywise.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.880
"ye-YEAH"

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.359
The turtle is a being of creation rather than
consumption, and actually vomited our universe

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.460
into reality when it had a stomach ache.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:50.300
So, you know...compared to that, a shapeshifting
death clown isn't all that weird after all.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:57.200
In the 2017 film, the story of It picks up
in 1988 when 7-year-old Georgie Denbrough

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.040
ventures out into a rainstorm to play with
a paper sailboat that his older brother Bill

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:01.720
made for him.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.960
The boat gets away from Georgie, and journeys
down the street until it ends up in a storm

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:06.460
drain.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:12.099
After chasing it, Georgie attempts to fish
the boat out, only to discover that -- uh-oh!

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.320
-- there's a clown in the storm drain as well.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.720
Though Georgie doesn't know it, this clown
is the evil entity Pennywise, who has just

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.820
awoken from its most recent 27-year slumber.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.830
Pennywise entices Georgie to come into the
sewer, promising not only to give him his

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:31.820
boat back, but also by telling him that an
entire circus is waiting for him in the sewer.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.780
Georgie gets a little suspicious, but he still
wants his boat back, so when Pennywise offers

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.700
it to him, the kid goes for it.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.480
And for his trouble, Georgie gets his arm
bitten off.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.420
Don't you just hate Mondays?

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.260
In the film, Georgie gets dragged into the
sewer, and the town eventually moves on, assuming

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.360
that he simply drowned.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:58.250
Bill, however, seems determined to find out
what really happened to his brother.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:03.690
The Losers' Club is the name of Bill's group
of friends who eventually go head to head

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:04.720
against Pennywise.

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:10.189
Along with Bill, the club's original members
are Richie Tozier, Eddie Kaspbrak, and Stan

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:15.600
Uris, with three more members added as the
story progresses: Ben Hanscom, Beverly Marsh,

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.260
and Mike Hanlon.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.720
Everyone in the group is an outcast in their
own way.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.180
The gang bonds over the fact that they're
all "losers" — hence the group's name — and

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:27.360
they spend the entire summer together following
Georgie's disappearance the previous fall.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:32.080
Though they aren't all aware of it initially,
a number of the Losers' Club are also suffering

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.600
from some sort of private trauma.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.040
Beverly's father is abusive and domineering,
not to mention creepy as hell.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.100
"Where are you sneaking off to?"

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:52.140
Eddie has an extremely overprotective mother
who makes him believe he's constantly sick.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:56.760
Mike is an orphan whose parents burned alive
— an event he witnessed firsthand.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.560
And Bill, of course, is still mourning the
disappearance of his little brother, for which

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.660
he feels guilty.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.200
A few of the Losers are more than just friends.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:06.840
Or, at least, they hope to be.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:11.340
Over the course of the first film, Ben and
Bill develop feelings for Beverly.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:16.019
Ben is the first to develop a major crush,
going so far as to write Beverly a love poem

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:17.100
from a secret admirer.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:22.160
Beverly cherishes the poem and comes to believe
Bill wrote it, and as a result, develops feelings

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.380
for him — much to Ben's chagrin.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.980
Even after discovering that Ben was her mystery
poet after he revives her from her Deadlights-induced

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:35.580
coma with a kiss, Beverly is unable to dismiss
her feelings for Bill, and at the conclusion

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.880
of the first film, Beverly and Bill kiss.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:43.720
In It: Chapter Two, fans can definitely expect
this love triangle to increase in importance.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.340
In the book, following the Losers' Club's
initial defeat of Pennywise, the group all

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:49.620
go their separate ways.

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:55.630
In the ensuing 27 years until Pennywise returns,
a number of them go through major changes,

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:57.020
but none greater than Ben.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:02.271
He physically transforms from an overweight
kid to a wealthy and attractive man, and Beverly

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.300
certainly notices when the gang reunites as
adults.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:09.520
After exploring some residual feelings for
Bill that include sleeping with him even though

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:14.120
they're both married, Beverly determines that
it's Ben for whom she really cares, and the

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:17.120
two of them finally end up together at the
novel's conclusion.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.560
In addition to Georgie, other children go
missing in Derry, such as Eddie Corcoran and

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:23.200
Betty Ripsom.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.940
This leads the Losers to believe that something
is amiss in their town.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:31.620
Before becoming a Loser himself, Ben begins
researching the town's history, and finds

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:37.020
that violent deaths and disappearances seem
to spike in the town every 27 years at a rate

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:38.580
six times the national average.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.200
"And that's just grown-ups.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:41.900
Kids are worse.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:44.500
Way, way worse."

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:49.380
But it's not always Pennywise who directly
causes the killings.

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:54.120
His evil influence infects the town whenever
he awakes, causing violence and hate crimes

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:54.760
to spike.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:59.340
In the novel, the role of town historian belongs
to Mike rather than Ben.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:04.080
Mike first learns of the town's sordid history
from his father, who kept a photo album filled

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.349
with pictures of Derry's history that consequently
features a number of photos of Pennywise;

00:06:09.349 --> 00:06:10.700
some from many decades earlier.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.200
As an adult, Mike is the only Loser to stay
behind in Derry after the rest of his friends

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.889
part ways, becoming the town librarian and
furthering his knowledge about the most messed

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:22.100
up little town in New England.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:26.140
"No one who dies here ever really dies."

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.300
One by one, the Losers draw the attention
of Pennywise.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.710
It appears to each of them separately, first
appearing as one of their worst fears before

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:41.260
showing them its clown form.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:46.380
Bill sees Georgie in his basement, Mike sees
burning bodies trying to escape a building,

00:06:46.389 --> 00:06:51.810
Eddie sees a grotesquely deformed leper, Stan
sees a painting of a ghoulish flautist come

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:56.570
to life, Beverly is sprayed by a geyser of
blood from her bathroom sink, Ben is chased

00:06:56.570 --> 00:07:01.460
by a headless man in the library, and Richie
sees a maggot-infested doll of his own corpse.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:05.311
Some of these encounters happen differently
in the book, but the upshot is the same: They

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:10.360
soon discover that each of their hallucinations
have one thing in common: a terrifying clown.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:14.940
The Losers quickly deduce that this clown
must be behind the child slayings in Derry.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:20.180
Pennywise also appears to Henry Bowers, the
vicious town bully who torments the Losers.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:24.200
But instead of eating him, Pennywise uses
Henry as an agent of destruction.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:27.860
So why go through all the trouble of turning
into a bunch of creepy monsters?

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.700
Well, Pennywise needs to eat humans to survive,
and It greatly prefers to devour children.

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:37.720
Its reason for this is pretty simple: According
to Pennywise, flesh tastes better when it's

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:41.190
seasoned with fear, and children are easier
to scare than adults.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:41.780
That's it.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:46.800
That's why Pennywise shapeshifts into whatever
its victims are most afraid of, and why it

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.160
usually stalks them for a time before killing
them.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:53.760
It wants them to be as scared as possible
before making them its next meal.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:56.740
In the book, It compares this process to salting
meat.

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:01.440
"And always let it rest afterwards so it relaxes,
becoming tender and juicy."

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:08.580
After a frightening encounter with It in the
abandoned house on Neibolt Street, most of

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:12.420
the Losers lose interest in trying to fight
Pennywise and just pretend like nothing ever

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:12.920
happened.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:17.680
That changes after Beverly is abducted by
the clown, which rallies the other Losers

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:18.860
to come to her rescue.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:23.720
They follow her trail to the town's sewers,
then enter It's domain to search for Beverly.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:28.680
Then, they take care of that mean old clown
once and for all...or so they think.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:33.960
After the traumatic experience of defeating
It, the children of the Losers' Club come

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.160
to the realization that they're growing up
fast.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:40.760
In the original film, after realizing the
importance of what they've just gone through,

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:45.370
Bill suggests the Losers make a blood oath
to swear that if Pennywise ever returns to

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:47.340
Derry, they'll return to defeat It again.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.110
He finds a piece of broken glass, cuts each
of their palms, and then they all stand in

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:53.180
a circle and hold hands.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:55.740
This scene essentially concludes the film.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:09:00.360
In the book, it's Stan rather than Bill who
initiates the blood oath, but the sentiment

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:01.220
remains the same.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:06.260
In It: Chapter Two and in the novel, all of
the Losers eventually move away from Derry

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:07.020
except for Mike.

00:09:07.269 --> 00:09:11.600
Twenty-seven years pass, and all of those
who left gradually forget the events of their

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:12.180
childhood.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.260
But Mike, having remained in Derry, remembers
everything.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:19.940
And when children start disappearing in the
town once again, he calls upon each of his

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:24.320
old friends to return to Derry and fulfill
the oath they made 27 years earlier.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:28.089
The Losers all find success in their adult
lives.

00:09:28.089 --> 00:09:31.910
But despite their professional successes,
some are still feeling the effects of their

00:09:31.910 --> 00:09:32.800
childhood trauma.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:35.660
But none are holding onto more trauma than
Stan.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:40.600
After receiving Mike's call, Stan immediately
remembers the harrowing events of his childhood.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:46.060
Not willing to face It again, he draws a bath
and slits his wrists, writing "IT" on the

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:48.810
wall in blood as his final act.

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:53.100
The other six Losers reunite at a Chinese
restaurant in Derry, the first time they've

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.520
all been at the same place in 27 years.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.120
After learning of Stan's death — and after
Pennywise makes its presence known by taking

00:10:00.130 --> 00:10:05.000
on various disgusting forms in the group's
fortune cookies — they declare their intent

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:06.950
to kill It once and for all.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:13.740
In the 2017 It film, Henry Bowers appeared
to die after Mike shoved him down a well.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:17.550
This posed a potential problem for the sequel
because in the book Henry goes on to play

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:18.780
a key role as an adult.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:22.560
Well, fans of the book can rest easy, because
Henry survived his fall.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:26.740
The adult version of the character has been
cast for the sequel, and he can even briefly

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:28.240
be seen in the movie's trailer.

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:33.020
After his final confrontation with the Losers
as a child, Henry is committed to a mental

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:34.800
institution for killing his father.

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:39.860
In the book, Pennywise again uses adult Henry
as a weapon against the Losers.

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:44.080
It frees Henry from his incarceration and
tells him to kill the Losers' Club.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:49.440
After siccing Henry on the Losers fails to
eliminate them, Pennywise hatches a new plan

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:50.280
to kill the group.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:55.260
In the book, Beverly's abusive husband Tom
arrives in Derry looking for his wife so he

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:55.780
can kill her.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:11:01.340
Pennywise takes control of Tom and has him
kidnap Bill's wife Audra, who had come to

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.160
Derry out of concern for her husband.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:06.560
Tom brings her to Its home in the sewers as
a lure to Bill.

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:11.880
Once there, It reveals its true form to Tom
and Audra, killing Tom and rendering Audra

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:12.380
comatose.

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:17.100
The lure works, and Bill heads to the sewer
for a final confrontation with It.

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:18.640
But he doesn't go alone.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:21.820
Ben, Beverly, Eddie, and Richie all accompany
him.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:26.820
These five members of the Losers' Club prove
to once again be too much for It, and after

00:11:26.830 --> 00:11:32.050
it battles them in the form of a giant spider,
the Losers kill It for good by besting it

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:33.940
in a sort of mystical battle of wills.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:36.120
"I believe in Santa Claus.

00:11:37.220 --> 00:11:38.700
I believe in the Easter Bunny."

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:45.110
Eddie dies during the fight, and the rest
of the Losers all again go their separate

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:48.540
ways — save for Ben and Beverly, who leave
Derry together.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.760
Eventually, they all forget the saga they've
been through — again.

